WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.560
When Xena first showed up as a character on&nbsp;
"Hercules: The Legendary Journeys," there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.560
was no way anyone could have predicted&nbsp;
that audiences would still be talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.880
about her decades later. How did this&nbsp;
spin-off become such a phenomenon? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.920
here’s everything you need to know&nbsp;
about “Xena: Warrior Princess.”

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.800
The character of Xena made her first&nbsp;
appearance on "Hercules: The Legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.640
Journeys" as a villainous warlord, out to kill&nbsp;
Kevin Sorbo's Hercules. Her arc was supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.920
to end with her death. Instead, the character&nbsp;
proved so popular that she earned a spin-off.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.880
In the "Hercules" episode "The Warrior Princess,"&nbsp;
Xena's fighting prowess is on full display,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.400
though her outfit is not yet the iconic one worn&nbsp;
in her series. Xena's goal is to seduce Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.840
Hurst's Iolaus, Hercules' best bud, in order to&nbsp;
get close enough to Herc to kill him. In her next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.880
episode, "The Gauntlet," she finally gets close to&nbsp;
Hercules, but in the final episode of her trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.160
"Unchained Heart," Xena reforms herself and goes&nbsp;
off to make amends for her treacherous past.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.960
This wasn't Lucy Lawless' first time on the show,&nbsp;
believe it or not. She'd already appeared in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.240
smaller role as Lyla, the wife of a centaur,&nbsp;
in the Season 1 episode "As Darkness Falls."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.320
Before that, she'd popped up as&nbsp;
Lysia in the 1994 pilot movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.160
"Hercules and the Amazon Women". When&nbsp;
the actress originally cast as Xena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.960
Vanessa Angel, became ill, Lawless got&nbsp;
the part. The rest is television history.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.200
Like "Hercules," its parent show, "Xena: Warrior&nbsp;
Princess" is steeped in Greek mythology. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.080
it's never said outright, it's certainly&nbsp;
implied that Xena might be a demi-goddess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.400
Her mother, Cyrene, shares a name with&nbsp;
a legendary water nymph. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.800
it's hinted in "The Furies" that Xena's&nbsp;
father is Ares, the Greek god of war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.360
Considering the often-flirtatious nature of&nbsp;
Ares and Xena's interactions, that would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.000
um, not great by current standards,&nbsp;
but totally in line with Greek myth.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.800
Xena being a demi-goddess would certainly&nbsp;
explain her beyond-human fighting feats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.080
Granted, cutting through dozens of soldiers with&nbsp;
apparent ease may merely mean Xena is super-agile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.160
and has a skilled fighting style. However,&nbsp;
Xena's ability to hold her own against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.200
various gods and goddesses she encounters and&nbsp;
frequently defeats puts her on par with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.320
fully-powered Hercules. Some godly parentage&nbsp;
would make that a whole lot more plausible.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.680
"Xena: Warrior Princess" began with a lot&nbsp;
of promise, having been spun-off from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.160
popular action-fantasy show which was&nbsp;
already solidly established. “Hercules”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.560
managed to head off competition from "Star&nbsp;
Trek: Deep Space Nine," and even topped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.640
"Baywatch." Even still, it didn't take&nbsp;
long for "Xena" to start surpassing it.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.840
"Xena" eventually joined "Hercules" in beating&nbsp;
out "Baywatch" in 1996. Besting "Hercules" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.960
something "Xena" kept doing, hitting a ratings&nbsp;
high of 7.8 the week of February 23rd,1997.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.240
Eventually, "Xena" even managed to&nbsp;
wrestle the top syndication spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.760
away from the ratings behemoth that&nbsp;
was "Star Trek: The Next Generation."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.080
As reported in “The Daily Universe,”&nbsp;
executive producer Rob Tapert told "TV Guide",

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.600
"Our initial goal was to bump off&nbsp;
'Baywatch' as the No. 2 show. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.000
is something I never expected to accomplish."

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.920
Like many shows, "Xena: Warrior Princess"&nbsp;
had its ups and downs in the ratings. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.880
it continued to maintain its&nbsp;
popularity over "Hercules".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.880
Not only did it last longer, its series&nbsp;
finale also scored higher ratings.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.480
Lucy Lawless met Rob Tapert, an executive&nbsp;
producer on "Hercules" and "Xena" after she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.000
was cast. The two were wed in 1998. But Lawless&nbsp;
and Tapert did not date right away; it took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.560
couple of years before they were even officially&nbsp;
an item. Still, Lawless told “People Magazine,”

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.960
“[Tapert] is the finest man I've ever known."

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.120
The pair have two sons, Julius and Judah.&nbsp;
The couple continues to collaborate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.640
projects. Lawless had roles in two&nbsp;
of Tapert's other television shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.680
"Spartacus: Blood and Sand"&nbsp;
and "Ash vs. Evil Dead."

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.040
"Xena: Warrior Princess" features plenty of&nbsp;
then-small-time actors who have gone on to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.440
big things. If you had to narrow the list down&nbsp;
to the two biggest actors to guest on "Xena:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.400
Warrior Princess," you would most certainly end&nbsp;
up with Bruce Campbell, who played Autolycus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.800
and Karl Urban, who played several roles&nbsp;
on the show, including Julius Caesar.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.360
Campbell, who is internationally renowned as&nbsp;
Ash Williams of the "Evil Dead" franchise, had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.400
a role in one of the most important scenes in the&nbsp;
show's history: Autolycus kisses Gabrielle while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.160
he's possessed by Xena. Campbell spoke fondly of&nbsp;
his time as Autolycus on the "Xena 25" podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.320
and expressed the belief that the show had more&nbsp;
of an impact than "Hercules" because of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:26.000
complexity. He expressed delight at seeing fans&nbsp;
dressed as Autolycus so many years later, saying,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.440
"Anything that has resonance decades&nbsp;
later [...] means it was worth doing."

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.680
Karl Urban has gone far since his early&nbsp;
days on "Xena:" He's beloved for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.760
work on "The Boys," and major franchises&nbsp;
including the “Marvel Cinematic Universe,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.160
"The Lord of the Rings," and "Star&nbsp;
Trek." Urban told “Whoosh.org” in 1998&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.640
that he enjoyed working on "Xena" and "Hercules"&nbsp;
due to the shows' environments, saying,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.600
"You get on set there and we&nbsp;
have fun. It's like a family."

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.000
When "Xena" began airing, fan&nbsp;
conventions were still fairly niche.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.880
So when “Creation Entertainment,” which has&nbsp;
been hosting fan conventions since 1971,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.920
held a small event in 1997 dedicated to&nbsp;
"Xena" and "Hercules," it was a big deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.360
When it was promoted to a "full scale touring&nbsp;
convention" in 1999, it was an even bigger deal.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.840
Guests at the first event included Lucy Lawless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.920
Kevin Sorbo, and Renée O'Connor. The&nbsp;
first two events proved so popular that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.400
“Creation” had to expand the convention to&nbsp;
larger venues to accommodate fan demand.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.160
“We do make a good team.”

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.520
As “The Los Angeles Times” reported in 1998,&nbsp;
"Hercules" fans were scarce compared to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.480
"Xenites," a fan-created term for "Xena"&nbsp;
lovers. Still, a good time was had by all.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.920
Among those at these events was&nbsp;
a then-unknown Ryan Gosling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.680
there to promote "Young Hercules,"&nbsp;
and Karl Urban covering The Beatles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.960
“Creation” alone could not satisfy the Xenites'&nbsp;
enthusiasm; several other "Xena" conventions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.840
events popped up across the United States,&nbsp;
everywhere from Las Vegas to New York City.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.080
Xena and Gabrielle's subtextual love story is a&nbsp;
big part of why "Xena" became such a sensation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.200
Though nothing was ever made irrefutably&nbsp;
explicit, that didn't stop the LGBTQ+ community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.520
from embracing the show wholeheartedly.&nbsp;
Even still, as “Pacific Standard” notes,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.160
“[Some fans] were disappointed that the Xena and&nbsp;
Gabrielle characters were never openly queer."

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.360
Still, it can't be denied that&nbsp;
"Xena" blazed a major trail.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.200
Though the show doesn't make Xena and&nbsp;
Gabrielle's romantic relationship canonical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.480
the current comic book series&nbsp;
has no such restraints. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.920
Xena and Gabrielle are portrayed as&nbsp;
being in a long-term relationship.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.000
“You are the most dear thing to me in the world.”

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.400
Lawless herself described&nbsp;
Xena as gay as early as 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.320
even deeming the pair to be essentially&nbsp;
married. For her part, Lawless has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.960
expressed delight at being considered a&nbsp;
gay icon. She told "Out Magazine" in 2003,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.760
"I've always been grateful to&nbsp;
[lesbian fans] for picking up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.000
on my show first. I feel they made it hip."

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:53.680
Renée O'Connor might be best known as Gabrielle,&nbsp;
but in fact, she's a Jill of all trades. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:59.520
directed two episodes of "Xena:" 1999's "Deja&nbsp;
Vu All Over Again" and 2001's "Dangerous Prey."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.000
Since her days on the show came to an end, she's&nbsp;
tapped into other talents, including producing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.160
stage acting, teaching, podcasting,&nbsp;
and art. Most prominently, O'Connor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.320
has her own company, “ROC Productions,”&nbsp;
which provides video production services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:18.080
and has produced films including 2017's&nbsp;
"Watch the Sky" and 2016's "The Usual."

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.200
O'Connor has quite a bit of&nbsp;
experience performing on stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.240
taking on such meaty roles as Lady Macbeth. She&nbsp;
also teaches classes on Shakespeare's monologues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.400
and on Sanford Meisner 's acting techniques.&nbsp;
Teaching has had to take a backseat recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.440
however, as O'Connor has focused on producing&nbsp;
a podcast on the culture of 1960s Austin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.560
On top of all of this, O'Connor is also a painter.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.680
Even with all that under her&nbsp;
belt, O'Connor has said she'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.400
play Gabrielle again if the show was ever&nbsp;
revived. In 2017, she told "Fox News",

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.760
"I would love to revisit these characters&nbsp;
again. [...] Obviously, it would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.600
completely different experience, but I think&nbsp;
it would be really interesting if that came up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.760
[and] if we could actually&nbsp;
play the same characters."

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.280
Xena's costume is a big part of what makes&nbsp;
her instantly recognizable. It's so iconic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.840
in fact, that it's displayed in two museums.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.480
One costume was donated in 2001 to the&nbsp;
Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.920
while Lawless donated the other to the Smithsonian&nbsp;
Institution's National Museum of American History.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:18.880
Xena's legendary look is detailed and&nbsp;
intricate, incorporating materials like leather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.920
mother of pearl, and brass. Barbara Darragh, the&nbsp;
costume's designer, says she took inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.080
from art nouveau, among other movements and&nbsp;
eras. One of the more interesting details of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.520
the costume is the size of the boots. Does Xena&nbsp;
have huge feet? Not exactly. The boots actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.840
contain running shoes to allow Lawless more&nbsp;
comfort and freedom of movement. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.000
this made up for the boning in the corset, which&nbsp;
was tight enough to make Lawless uncomfortable.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.280
Though she's proficient&nbsp;
with just about everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.520
Xena's favorite weapon is her chakram . She&nbsp;
gains multiple chakram throughout the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.360
including the Balanced Chakram, which&nbsp;
can break into two halves to be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.080
separately. Though Xena's magical weapon is&nbsp;
fictional, the chakram is actually a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.040
South Asian weapon. It’s said to be wielded&nbsp;
by Vishnu, one of Hinduism's major deities.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.840
In “Entertainment Weekly's”&nbsp;
oral "herstory" of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:11.200
Rob Tapert recalled director Doug Lefler coming up&nbsp;
with the signature weapon rather casually, saying,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.960
"[Lefler] said, 'Hey, this is the object she&nbsp;
should have when we find her in 'Hercules.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.600
This round throwing object that shocked them.&nbsp;
This should be the Warrior Princess' weapon.'"

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.440
Although the show's chakram were mostly&nbsp;
harmless props, often made of rubber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.400
to be more easily tossed around, one metal&nbsp;
chakram prop was actually dangerous. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.880
inlaid stones were made of abalone,&nbsp;
which emits a toxic dust when ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.360
Real world chakram may not fly as magically as&nbsp;
they do on the show, but they sure can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:41.760
kill a person if they're made of abalone and&nbsp;
assembled in an area without proper ventilation!

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.520
A whole lot of care went into the&nbsp;
development of Xena's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:49.840
from what she wears to who she loves&nbsp;
to her unforgettable battle cry.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.400
As Lucy Lawless told “PeopleTV,” her future&nbsp;
husband, Rob Tapert, decided that Xena needed a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:04.160
quote, “signature call, like Tarzan." Right around&nbsp;
then, Lawless saw a funeral on TV where Arab women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:09.120
were ululating: a type of trilling howl used&nbsp;
to express strong emotion. In Xena's case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.240
it's used as a clear and intimidating war-cry.&nbsp;
Lawless couldn't quite mimic what she saw on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:19.600
so she tweaked it into her own thing. Thus,&nbsp;
Xena gained her very own Tarzan-style howl.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.000
The ululation Lawless heard that fateful&nbsp;
day has a name. It's called the zaghrouta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.840
and can be heard throughout the Middle East.&nbsp;
It isn't restricted to funerals, and can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:33.360
heard during happy occasions including weddings,&nbsp;
graduations. It was even used by pop star Shakira,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.760
who introduced much of the world to the zaghrouta&nbsp;
during the 2020 Super Bowl halftime show.

